course: technology
lesson: 10
title: Keeping cold: fridges, freezers and heat pumps
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A fridge does not make cold: it moves heat out of the box and lets it go from pipes at the back, which is why the back of a fridge feels warm.
explain: A pump called the compressor pushes a special liquid round pipes; inside the fridge it turns into a gas and soaks up heat, the way drying sweat cools your skin, and outside it gives the heat away.
explain: The humming you hear in the kitchen is the compressor switching on, and the quiet is it resting when the inside is cold enough.
explain: Cold matters because germs grow slowly below about 5 degrees and stop growing in a freezer at about minus 18, so milk and meat keep much longer.
explain: An air conditioner is the same trick for a room, and a heat pump is a fridge turned backwards: it pulls heat out of cold winter air and pumps it into the house.
fact: A fridge moves heat out rather than making cold: a special liquid turns to gas in pipes inside and soaks up heat, and the heat is let out from pipes at the back.
fact: The humming noise is the compressor, a small pump, switching on and off to keep the inside cold; a fridge is one of the few machines in a house that runs day and night.
fact: A freezer works like a fridge but colder, around minus 18 degrees, so germs stop growing and food keeps for months.
fact: Freezing does not kill most germs; they wake up again when the food thaws.
fact: A small switch turns on the fridge light when the door opens and off when it shuts, and a rubber seal with magnets inside keeps the cold in; a piece of paper shut in the door should be hard to pull out.
fact: Food stays safer in a fridge kept below about 5 degrees, because germs grow slowly in the cold, so leftovers go in once they have cooled.
fact: Each time the door is open, cold air spills out at the bottom, so the door should not be left open; an open fridge cannot cool the kitchen, because it works harder and lets out even more heat at the back, so the room gets warmer.
fact: Water gets bigger when it freezes, which is why a full glass bottle can crack in the freezer.
fact: Rules: never climb inside a fridge or freezer, because you could get trapped, and never put ice or metal straight from the freezer on your lips or tongue, because it can stick and hurt.
fact: A cool box has thick foam walls, mostly trapped still air, which slow heat getting in, and its ice packs soak up heat as they melt.
fact: An air conditioner works like a fridge for a room, taking heat from inside and pushing it out through a box outside; the first modern one was built in 1902 to stop paper wrinkling in a print shop.
fact: Air conditioners drip water outside because cooling the air turns some of its water vapour (water as an invisible gas in the air) into droplets.
fact: A heat pump works like a fridge backwards: it collects heat from the outside air or the ground, even in winter, because even cold air holds some heat, and pumps it into the house.
fact: Open supermarket chillers blow a curtain of cold air down the front to keep most of the cold in, and adding doors to them saves a lot of electricity.
exercise: What does a fridge really do? | It moves heat out of the box | It makes cold out of nothing | It blows in cold air from outside | It turns the food slowly into ice
exercise: Why does the back of a fridge feel warm? | The heat taken from inside is let out there | The fridge is about to break and is overheating | The light bulb is at the back | The food warms it from inside
exercise: What happens to the special liquid in the pipes inside the fridge? | It turns into gas and soaks up heat | It freezes into solid ice | It drips down slowly onto the food on the shelves | It boils away and is gone for ever
exercise: What makes the fridge hum? | The compressor, a small pump, switching on | The light bulb buzzing | The food settling on the shelves | Water dripping in the pipes
exercise: Why does the fridge hum, stop and then hum again? | The pump runs until it is cold enough, rests, and starts again as it warms | It is slowly breaking down | It is talking to the freezer | It hums only when someone walks past
exercise: About how cold is a home freezer? | Around minus 18 degrees | Around plus 5 degrees | Exactly zero degrees | Around minus 100 degrees
exercise: Does freezing kill most germs? | No, they stop growing and wake up when the food thaws | Yes, every germ dies at once | Yes, after about an hour | No, germs grow even faster in the cold than in the warm
exercise: What keeps the cold in at the fridge door? | A rubber seal with magnets inside | A heavy metal lock that clicks shut | A thin curtain of very cold running water | A thick wooden frame round the door
exercise: How can you test the fridge door seal? | Shut a piece of paper in the door; it should be hard to pull out | Shine a torch at the door | Listen for a whistle from the door | Hold an ice cube against the door
exercise: Below about what temperature should a fridge keep food? | About 5 degrees | About 25 degrees | About 50 degrees | About 15 degrees
exercise: Where does the cold air spill out when the fridge door is open? | At the bottom | At the very top | Out through the back | Nowhere, cold air cannot move
exercise: Why can a full glass bottle crack in the freezer? | Water gets bigger when it freezes | Glass shrinks to nothing in the cold | The freezer squeezes everything inside | Ice is heavier than glass
exercise: How does a cool box keep things cold? | Foam walls slow heat getting in and ice packs soak up heat | A small battery inside it makes cold air and blows it all round | Foam is naturally freezing cold | Its lid is filled with snow
exercise: How does an air conditioner cool a room? | It takes heat from inside and pushes it out through a box outside | It makes snow and blows it in | It sucks most of the air out of the room so that no heat can stay there | It sprays cold water on the walls
exercise: Why was the first modern air conditioner built in 1902? | To stop paper wrinkling in a print shop | To cool a king's palace | To freeze ice cream for a fair | To cool racing cars
exercise: Why does an air conditioner drip water outside? | Cooling the air turns some of its water vapour into droplets | It leaks the drinking water | It is washing itself | Rain gets inside the box
exercise: How does a heat pump warm a house? | It collects heat from the outside air or ground and pumps it inside | It burns gas like a fire | It makes heat out of nothing | It stores summer sunshine in a box
exercise: Why don't open supermarket chillers lose all their cold? | A curtain of cold air blows down the front | Shoppers' cold hands keep the shelves chilled | The food on the shelves is frozen solid all the way | The shelves themselves are made out of thick ice
test: «hide and seek, gonna hide in the chest freezer, no one'll find me» What is right? | Never climb into a freezer or fridge, you could get trapped | Fine, if you keep the lid open a crack | Fine, but only for a few minutes | Fine, freezers always open easily from the inside if you push
test: «lick the metal ice tray straight out the freezer, dare ya» What is the safe answer? | Don't: frozen metal can stick to your tongue and hurt | Do it, cold metal cannot stick to skin | Licking warms the metal, so it is safe | Only plastic trays can stick to tongues, metal ones never can
test: «boiling hot, leave the fridge open to cool the kitchen» What would really happen? | The kitchen would get warmer, because the fridge dumps its heat out the back while working harder | The kitchen would cool like an air conditioner | The fridge would fill the kitchen with snow | Nothing would change at all
test: «fridge is broke, the back's all warm!» What is the clever answer? | A warm back is normal: that is where the heat from inside goes | Yes, a warm back always means the motor is broken and needs fixing | The fridge is about to catch fire | The food is cooking the fridge
test: «how can u heat a house with freezing air, that's mental» What is right? | Even cold air holds some heat, and a heat pump collects it and pumps it in | You can't, heat pumps only ever work in summer, when the air is already warm | Heat pumps burn the cold air for fuel | Heat pumps melt ice to make fire
test: What would happen to milk left on the kitchen table all day instead of in the fridge? | Germs would grow faster and it would go off sooner | It would stay fresh for even longer out in the open air | It would slowly turn into safe yoghurt by the evening | Nothing at all, milk never goes off however warm it is
test: «stick a full glass bottle of juice in the freezer to chill it quick» What could go wrong? | The juice gets bigger as it freezes and can crack the bottle | The glass bottle will melt | The juice will turn into fizzy pop | Nothing can ever go wrong
test: «frozen food lasts for ever cos freezing kills germs» What is right? | Freezing stops germs growing, but they wake up when the food thaws | Yes, every germ dies in the freezer | Frozen food goes off within a day, so it must be eaten straight away | Germs grow faster when frozen
test: An ice tray has 2 rows of 8 cubes. You use 5. How many cubes are left? | 11 | 13 | 16 | 3
test: Each cool box needs 4 ice packs. How many packs for 3 cool boxes? | 12 | 7 | 16 | 9
test: «does the fridge light stay on all night with the door shut?» What is right? | No, a small switch turns it off when the door shuts | Yes, it stays on all the time, even when nobody opens it | Only at weekends, when the family is home more often | It stays on until all the food inside it has been eaten
test: «shop fridge has no door, it'll lose all the cold, thick design» What is right? | A curtain of cold air keeps most cold in, though doors save even more electricity | Open chillers lose no cold at all | The cold leaks out only at night | The chillers are packed with ice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a domestic refrigerator? | It transfers heat from its interior and releases it via pipes at the rear | It manufactures cold within its motor and expels it into its interior via a fan | It admits cold air from outside the dwelling | It cools the food by causing the food itself to evaporate
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a full glass bottle of water may crack within a freezer. | Water expands upon freezing | Glass contracts to nothing at low temperatures | Ice is heavier than glass | The freezer compresses all its contents
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning food stored in a freezer. | The growth of germs is arrested, though they may resume upon thawing | All germs are destroyed instantaneously | The food becomes permanently free of germs, even after it has been thawed | Germs multiply more rapidly at low temperatures
exam: Ascertain the number of ice cubes remaining in a tray of three rows of six cubes, it being given that seven cubes have been removed. | Eleven | Eighteen | Thirteen | Ten
